---
title: 'Job Application Tracker: How to Keep Track of Every Application'
publishedAt: 2026-11-27
type: job-seekers
description: >-
  What to record for each job application, which statuses to use, when to follow
  up, and how to track jobs from any site in one board.
---
**Quick answer:** To keep track of job applications, record each one in a single place with the company, role, link, date applied, current status, next follow-up date and any contact. Update it whenever something changes and review it once a week. On GetHired, the **My Jobs** page does this as a board or a list. Applications you send through GetHired appear automatically, and you can add jobs from any other site by pasting the link or typing the details.

After a couple of weeks of applying, it gets hard to answer simple questions. Did I already apply to this company? Which resume did I send? Who haven't I heard from? A tracker answers those in seconds.

## What should you track for each job application?

{% table %}
-
- Field
- Why it matters
---
- Company and role
- Stops you applying twice
---
- Link to the posting
- Lets you reread it before an interview
---
- Date applied
- Tells you when to follow up
---
- Status
- Shows what's still alive
---
- Follow-up date
- Turns "I should chase that" into a task
---
- Resume used
- So your interview answers match what they read
---
- Contact
- The recruiter or the person who referred you
---
- Notes
- Interview questions, salary discussed, next steps
{% /table %}

Save the posting's key requirements in your notes too. Listings are often taken down while you're still interviewing.

## Which statuses should you use?

Keep them few enough that updating takes seconds. GetHired uses these:

{% table %}
-
- Board column
- What it means
---
- Applied
- Sent, no reply yet
---
- In review
- The company has responded or is considering you
---
- Interviewing
- You have an interview scheduled or in progress
---
- Offer
- You have an offer
---
- Closed
- Accepted, rejected, withdrawn or ghosted
{% /table %}

Jobs you haven't applied to yet don't belong on the board. They sit on a separate **Saved** tab, so your list of options never gets mixed up with your list of applications.

## How does the GetHired application tracker work?

Open [My Jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/my-jobs). It has two tabs, **Applications** and **Saved**, and applications can be shown as a board or as a sortable list. One search box finds a job by company or title.

### Applications you send through GetHired

These appear on their own, and the company's status changes show up on your tracker. Because the company updates them, you can't drag these cards, but you can add your own private notes and a follow-up date. The company can't see your notes.

### Jobs you applied to somewhere else

Use **Add job**. Paste a link and the form is filled in from the page where it can be read. Some sites block this, including some large job boards. In that case the form opens with your link saved and you type the rest.

When you click through to apply on another site from a GetHired listing, you're asked whether you applied. Say yes and it's added to your tracker.

### Jobs you're still considering

Save them while you search. When you apply, choose **Mark as applied** and the job moves to your Applications tab.

## How do you know when to follow up?

Set a follow-up date as soon as you apply or finish an interview. Your [dashboard](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/dashboard) lists what needs attention: follow-ups that are due, applications with no reply, and recent updates from companies.

A reasonable default:

- **After applying:** follow up in 7 to 10 days if you have a real contact.
- **After an interview:** send a thank-you within a day, then follow the timeline they gave you.
- **If they gave a decision date:** wait until a few days after it.

Our [follow-up email templates](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-follow-up-on-a-job-application-with-email-templates-and-timelines) cover what to write.

## What does "ghosted" mean, and when should you mark it?

An application is ghosted when it goes silent and you no longer expect a reply. On GetHired, if an application you added yourself has had no status change for 30 days while still in Applied or In review, the tracker asks whether to mark it as ghosted. It never changes a status on its own.

Closing dead applications matters. It keeps the board honest and shows you how many opportunities are really active.

## How can your tracker improve your job search?

Once you have a couple of dozen applications, look for patterns:

- **Few replies at all?** Your resume or targeting needs work. Try [tailoring your resume to each job](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-tailor-your-resume-for-each-job-without-making-things-up).
- **Replies but no interviews after the first call?** Work on how you introduce your experience.
- **Interviews but no offers?** [Practise with a mock interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-practise-for-interviews-with-an-ai-mock-interview).
- **Not applying often enough?** Set a [weekly application goal](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-many-jobs-should-you-apply-to-per-week).

## Spreadsheet or application tracker?

{% table %}
-
- Task
- Spreadsheet
- GetHired My Jobs
---
- Add an application
- Type every field
- Automatic for GetHired applications, or paste a link
---
- Company status changes
- You find out and edit the row
- Shown automatically for GetHired applications
---
- Follow-up reminders on screen
- Only if you build them
- Listed on your dashboard
---
- Ask questions about your search
- Filter and sort by hand
- Ask the agent in plain English
{% /table %}

A spreadsheet is fine if you like it and keep it current. The best tracker is the one you'll actually update.

## Frequently asked questions

### Can I track jobs I applied to outside GetHired?

Yes. Add a job from any company site or job board with a link or by typing the details, then update its status as you go.

### Can companies see my tracker?

No. Jobs you add yourself, and your private notes and follow-up dates, aren't shown to companies.

### Can I update my tracker by chatting?

Yes. The GetHired agent can list your applications and saved jobs, add a job, and update the status, notes or follow-up date of applications you added.

### How often should I update my job application tracker?

Add each application when you send it, change the status when you hear something, and spend ten minutes once a week reviewing follow-ups.

[Open your application tracker](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/my-jobs) and add the applications you're already waiting on.
